@page "/pitch"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<h1>Pitch and frequency</h1>
<p class="lead">
	A note is a vibration, and its pitch is how fast it vibrates. This page shows the arithmetic that turns "the A above middle C" into 440 vibrations per second,
	and every other note into a number.
</p>

<h2>1. An octave is a doubling</h2>
<p>
	Raise a note by an <strong>octave</strong> and its frequency doubles: 220 Hz, 440 Hz, 880 Hz. Our ears hear equal <em>ratios</em> as equal steps, which is why the
	scale is exponential, not linear. The step from 220 to 440 sounds the same size as 440 to 880 even though the second is twice as many hertz.
</p>

<h2>2. Twelve equal steps</h2>
<p>
	Western music divides the octave into 12 semitones of equal ratio <em>r</em>. Twelve steps must multiply to 2, so <em>r</em><sup>12</sup> = 2 and
</p>
<p class="formula">r = 2<sup>1/12</sup> &asymp; 1.05946,&emsp; f(m) = 440 &middot; 2<sup>(m &minus; 69)/12</sup> Hz</p>
<p>
	where <em>m</em> is the MIDI note number (69 is the A above middle C, 60 is middle C). Each semitone raises the frequency by about 5.9%.
	Move the slider to try it:
</p>

<div class="card">
	<div class="row">
		<label>MIDI note <strong>@note</strong>
			<input type="range" min="24" max="108" step="1" @bind="note" @bind:event="oninput" />
		</label>
		<label>detune <strong>@cents</strong> cents
			<input type="range" min="-50" max="50" step="1" @bind="cents" @bind:event="oninput" />
		</label>
		<button @onclick="PlayAsync">Play it</button>
	</div>
	<div class="grid" style="margin-top:0.5rem">
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@Pitch.NameOf(note)</div><p>note name (scientific pitch)</p></div>
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@Frequency.ToString("0.00") Hz</div><p>f = 440 &middot; 2<sup>(@(note - 69 + cents / 100.0) / 12)</sup></p></div>
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@((Frequency / Pitch.FrequencyOf(note)).ToString("0.0000"))&times;</div><p>ratio to the in-tune note (1 when detune is 0)</p></div>
	</div>
	@if (audioUrl is not null)
	{
		<audio controls autoplay src="@audioUrl" style="width:100%;margin-top:0.75rem"></audio>
	}
</div>

<h2>3. Cents</h2>
<p>
	A <strong>cent</strong> is one hundredth of a semitone, a ratio of 2<sup>1/1200</sup> &asymp; 1.00058. Most people cannot hear a difference below about 5 to 10 cents.
	Each generated song is detuned from concert pitch by up to 45 cents, so that two songs in the same key still have slightly different frequencies, just as
	two real recordings made on different instruments or tape machines would.
</p>

<h2>4. Harmonics: why a note is not a single frequency</h2>
<p>
	A real instrument playing 220 Hz also produces 440, 660, 880 Hz and so on: whole-number multiples called <strong>harmonics</strong>. The mix of their loudnesses is the
	<em>timbre</em>. The generator builds each note by adding sine waves,
</p>
<p class="formula">x(t) = &sum;<sub>h=1</sub><sup>H</sup> &rho;<sup>h&minus;1</sup> &middot; sin(2&pi; &middot; h &middot; f &middot; t)</p>
<p>
	with &rho; between 0.35 and 0.85 (the song's "brightness": bigger means more upper harmonics). The tune uses 6 harmonics; the pad and bass use 3. Here is the harmonic series for
	the note you chose, with each harmonic's weight:
</p>

<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Harmonic h</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Weight &rho;<sup>h&minus;1</sup> (&rho; = 0.6)</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		@for (var h = 1; h <= 6; h++)
		{
			<tr><td>@h</td><td>@((Frequency * h).ToString("0.0")) Hz</td><td>@Math.Pow(0.6, h - 1).ToString("0.000")</td></tr>
		}
	</tbody>
</table>

@code {
	private IJSObjectReference? module;
	private int note = 69;
	private int cents;
	private string? audioUrl;

	private double Frequency => Pitch.FrequencyOf(note + (cents / 100.0));

	private async Task PlayAsync()
	{
		const int rate = 22050;
		var samples = new float[rate * 2];
		var frequency = Frequency;
		for (var i = 0; i < samples.Length; i++)
		{
			var t = (double)i / rate;
			var envelope = Math.Min(1, t * 80) * Math.Exp(-1.6 * t);
			var sum = 0.0;
			for (var h = 1; h <= 6; h++)
			{
				if (frequency * h < rate / 2.0)
				{
					sum += Math.Pow(0.6, h - 1) * Math.Sin(2 * Math.PI * h * frequency * t);
				}
			}

			samples[i] = (float)(0.4 * envelope * sum);
		}

		module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/music.js");
		audioUrl = await module.InvokeAsync<string>("createAudioUrl", WavEncoder.Encode(samples, rate));
	}

	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		if (module is not null)
		{
			await module.DisposeAsync();
		}
	}
}
